# Per call observer cost within a fixed accounting Job

This bounded local attribution compares the installed `profiled` path against
ordinary `Command::output()` while keeping the same unlimited private Job and
all original worker, identity, byte, terminal-census and time checks. It can
locate the additional cost of per-call retained-handle observation plus its
phase serialization. It cannot measure the cost of Job membership itself or
isolate a pure Win32 counter-call cost from scheduling and useful Git work.

The unchanged four-call Rust worker is reused. Reference/profile do not alter
Git argv, cwd, environment, stdin or pipe semantics. Independent archived body
bytes and before/after Git file pins remain mandatory. The
[protocol](git-cpu-overhead-protocol.toml) fixes four sequential blocks:
reference/profile/profile/reference, then its reverse, alternating twice.
There are sixteen workers and sixty-four ordinary short Git commands, not a
repeat of the old 96-call pipe cohort or V6. Commit the exact source/protocol and
build receipt before one attribution attempt. Never retry a refused cohort.

One common 120-second origin starts before API/Job creation and is not renewed
for each worker. Each failure keeps its first refusal and has the unchanged
five-second cleanup window for only its own Job. First failure stops the cohort;
do not summarize a successful subset. Four-GiB free-space and 16-MiB per-worker
log bounds, original-handle custody and all terminal checks remain. Source/HEAD,
compiled worker and Git image pins must remain unchanged through postflight.

For every worker, record all-member lifetime Job CPU, original worker CPU and
external coordinator CPU. Monotonic wall/coordinator CPU span one complete frame
from before Job creation through worker/Job handle close, including read/parse
and validation, but excluding the subsequent sample-file append. Whole Job CPU
includes native Git; reference has no per-call launcher counters, so do not
invent per-call or native-only CPU for it. Profile residuals keep their prior
non-launcher-descendant label and are not the comparison's CPU denominator.

Retain both raw samples per arm in every block. Sum each pair and report exact
signed deltas and ratio numerator/denominator for each of the four blocks; a
zero reference CPU counter yields an explicit unavailable ratio. No trimming,
discarding the first sample, retry, aggregate confidence claim or acceptance
floor is introduced. Four local blocks are exploratory attribution, not an
instrumentation acceptance gate: `overhead_accepted`, `speedup_established` and
`package_pass` remain false regardless of the observed differences. Negative
deltas remain negative rather than being called negative overhead. Neither
these data nor old timing intervals authorize a product change or a longer
package deadline. No server, service, clock or dependency action is included.
